    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes \u2014 **71 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp59Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe specification is satisfied. Verifying the codebase, the RFC 7807 problem-detail behavior for all rejection responses is implemented in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`:\n\n- **Every 400/409/429 rejection handler** builds its response via `detailBuild(...)`, which produces a Spring `ProblemDetail` populated with all four required members:\n  - `type` \u2014 set to the request URL (`problemDetail.setType(...)`)\n  - `title` \u2014 the exception's simple class name\n  - `status` \u2014 set via `ProblemDetail.forStatus(status)`\n  - `detail` \u2014 a human-readable message per exception type\n- Each handler returns `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, and Spring's message-handling automatically emits the `application/problem+json` content type for a `ProblemDetail` body \u2014 which is exactly what `Cp59Tests` asserts (`contentTypeCompatibleWith(\"application/problem+json\")` + `$.status == 400`).\n- The HTTP status codes are unchanged: the handlers still return `BAD_REQUEST` (400), `CONFLICT` (409), and `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) respectively.\n\nAll 400/409/429 rejection paths in the app (`MethodArgumentNotValid`, `RequiredFieldsMissing`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `DuplicateEmail/Identity/Telephone/Household`, `CityAtCapacity`, `DailyOwnerLimitExceeded`, `FutureRegistrationDate`) route through this same `ProblemDetail` mechanism, so they all return the required `application/problem+json` body. No source changes were needed to satisfy the spec, and the test suite is green.",
